the nikon revolution pro controller is  one of the high-end alternatives for the  ps4 it offers several enhancements and  customizable functions but it does have  a few drawbacks the revolution is one of  two pro-style controllers licensed by  Sony but it's only available in European  markets so you'll have trouble getting  your hands on it if you're in the US it  costs around 90 British pounds which  converts to about 115 US dollars the  other Sony licensed controllers the  Razer Rhys U which is only available  overseas as well if you want to know how  brazzers controller performs check out  our review after this video and  subscribe to our channel for more but  for now let me break down the nikon  revolution the body has a sleek matte  finish with a strong grip that isn't  sticky despite it looking a bit heftier  it's fairly light weighing about the  same as the DualShock 4 adjustable  weights are also packaged which slip  into either side of the handles if you  prefer something heavier one thing you  must know this controller is not  Wireless it only works through a USB  cable with a proprietary 5 pin end a  metal screw cap keeps the cables secure  the enlarged face buttons stand out  immediately and they feel similar to the  stock Xbox one controller buttons which  isn't a bad thing there sighs neither  helped nor hinder our gameplay  experience the analog sticks are light  to the touch and offer consistent fluid  movement it's a significant improvement  over the DualShock for helping precise  aiming and overall ease of use it's also  the only ps4 controller we tested that  takes on the stick placement of the Xbox  controllers the revolution features a  disc like eight way directional pad that  felt responsive it's the best of any ps4  controller the d-pad sees limited use in  most games but it works great for  fighting games though series players  would naturally still opt for a fight  stick while l1 and r1 have a light touch  in a satisfying tactile feel the l2 and  r2 triggers leave a lot to be desired  they feel stiff and squishy and require  more force than necessary to hold down  which is a detriment in racing games and  shooters the triggers are also shaped in  a strange way that forces you to hold  the controller in a position that feels  awkward you'll be in a dilemma  choosing what's comfortable to hold and  what's comfortable for using the  triggers there are two pedals on the  back and each paddle acts as two  separate buttons depending on the way  the pressed the our buttons labeled m3  and m4 are responsive and effortless to  pull even in the most frantic situations  but not to the point where they can be  mistakenly pressed down and they're  sensible placement makes for the most  natural feeling paddle type buttons to  use since they reside right where the  middle finger is on the handles however  the inner buttons m1 and m2 are  inconsistent and tough to use making  them more trouble than they're worth  still two extra buttons is better than  none make on also stands out with its  robust software for full controller  customization even allowing you to tweak  the littlest things like the right  sticks response curve however this  requires a PC there isn't a way to  program the extra buttons on the fly  either  but you can save for profiles to the  controller and swap between them at any  time  now that you're aware of every little  detail of the nikon revolution if I want  to know how all of its features come  together when actually playing games  the precise analog sticks really shine  in titanfall too making it easier to  land shots and keep track of moving  targets and the m3 and m4 buttons made  jumping and connecting wall runs more  fluid which is a significant advantage  however and the triggers were tiring to  use deeper into play sessions and their  shape didn't stop nagging many of the  same advantages translated to overwatch  as always bunny hopping and using  ultimate abilities were easy to pull off  and using m3 and m4 buttons but the  triggers were even more tiring to using  this time around especially with  characters that you semi-auto weapons  like Farah and junk rack spamming the  triggers can wear you out in a longer  session giving the trigger stiffness the  main appeal of using a premium  controller in single player games would  be to make life a little bit easier by  mapping the dodge action to m3 or m4 in  both blood-borne and horizon zero dawn  escaping enemies and avoiding damage  while moving the camera at all times  make gameplay more fluid the placement  of those buttons also made these actions  feel natural  there's a lot to like about the Nikon  revolution from its fluid analog sticks  to some smartly placed buttons but  overall it's held back by some curious  design decisions like the trigger feel  and the trigger shape it's ultimately  too hard to ignore we've reviewed a few  other ps4 controllers so do yourself a  favor and check out what we thought of  razors and scuff gaming controllers and  as always if you liked this video give  it a thumbs up and don't forget to  subscribe to GameSpot thanks for  watching
 

